<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-inverses-solve-sys">
<title>Solving systems using inverses</title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case,
	solve the system using the inverse of the corresponding coefficient matrix.
</p></introduction>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-inverses-solve-sys-2x2-1a">

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcr}
			  x \amp - \amp   y \amp = \amp  0 \\
			3 x \amp - \amp 2 y \amp = \amp -8
		\end{sysofeqns}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		This system is represented in matrix form <m> A \uvec{x} = \uvec{b} </m> where
		<md><mrow>
			A \amp =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				1 \amp -1 \\
				3 \amp -2
			\end{abmatrix}
			\text{,}
			\amp
			\uvec{x} \amp = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} \text{,} \amp
			\uvec{b} \amp = \begin{abmatrix}{r} 0 \\ -8 \end{abmatrix} \text{.}
		</mrow></md>
		The coefficient matrix is identical to the matrix of <xref ref="exercise-inverses-2x2-1" text="type-local" /> and has inverse
		<md>
			\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				-2 \amp 1 \\
				-3 \amp 1
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>.
		The system therefore has unique solution
		<md>
			\uvec{x} =
			\inv{A} \uvec{b} =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				-2 \amp 1 \\
				-3 \amp 1
			\end{abmatrix}
			\begin{abmatrix}{r} 0 \\ -8 \end{abmatrix}
			= \begin{bmatrix} -8 \\ -8 \end{bmatrix}
		</md>.
		In this unique solution, <m>x = -8</m> and <m>y = -8</m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-inverses-solve-sys-2x2-1b">

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcr}
			  x \amp - \amp   y \amp = \amp 6 \\
			3 x \amp - \amp 2 y \amp = \amp 5
		\end{sysofeqns}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		This system has the same coefficient matrix and variable column as <xref ref="exercise-inverses-solve-sys-2x2-1a" text="type-local" />,
		but has column of constants
		<md> \uvec{b} = \begin{bmatrix} 6 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix} </md>.
		We can use the same inverse matrix computed in <xref ref="exercise-inverses-2x2-1" text="type-local" /> to compute the unique solution
		<md>
			\uvec{x} =
			\inv{A} \uvec{b} =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				-2 \amp 1 \\
				-3 \amp 1
			\end{abmatrix}
			\begin{abmatrix}{r} 6 \\ 5 \end{abmatrix}
			= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 8 \end{bmatrix}
		</md>.
		In this unique solution, <m>x = 1</m> and <m>y = 8</m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-inverses-solve-sys-2x2-2a">

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcr}
			 2 s \amp - \amp 3 t \amp = \amp -7 \\
			-2 s \amp + \amp 4 t \amp = \amp  3
		\end{sysofeqns}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		This system is represented in matrix form <m> A \uvec{x} = \uvec{b} </m> where
		<md><mrow>
			A \amp =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				 2 \amp -3 \\
				-2 \amp  4
			\end{abmatrix}
			\text{,}
			\amp
			\uvec{x} \amp = \begin{bmatrix} s \\ t \end{bmatrix} \text{,} \amp
			\uvec{b} \amp = \begin{abmatrix}{r} -7 \\ 3 \end{abmatrix} \text{.}
		</mrow></md>
		The coefficient matrix is identical to the matrix of <xref ref="exercise-inverses-2x2-3" text="type-local" /> and has inverse
		<md>
			\begin{bmatrix}
				2 \amp \frac{3}{2} \\
				1 \amp      1
			\end{bmatrix}
		</md>.
		The system therefore has unique solution
		<md>
			\uvec{x} =
			\inv{A} \uvec{b} =
			\begin{bmatrix}
				2 \amp \frac{3}{2} \\
				1 \amp      1
			\end{bmatrix}
			\begin{abmatrix}{r} -7 \\ 3 \end{abmatrix}
			= \begin{abmatrix}{r} -\frac{19}{2} \\ -4 \end{abmatrix}
		</md>.
		In this unique solution, <m>s = -19/2</m> and <m>t = -4</m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-inverses-solve-sys-2x2-2b">

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcr}
			 2 x_1 \amp - \amp 3 x_2 \amp = \amp 3 \\
			-2 x_1 \amp + \amp 4 x_2 \amp = \amp 4
		\end{sysofeqns}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		This system has the same coefficient matrix as <xref ref="exercise-inverses-solve-sys-2x2-2a" text="type-local" />,
		but has variable and constant columns
		<md><mrow>
			\uvec{x} \amp = \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \end{bmatrix} \text{,}
			\amp
			\uvec{b} \amp = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix} \text{.}
		</mrow></md>
		We can use the same inverse matrix computed in <xref ref="exercise-inverses-2x2-3" text="type-local" /> to compute the unique solution
		<md>
			\uvec{x} =
			\inv{A} \uvec{b} =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				-2 \amp 1 \\
				-3 \amp 1
			\end{abmatrix}
			\begin{abmatrix}{r} 3 \\ 4 \end{abmatrix}
			= \begin{bmatrix} 12 \\ 7 \end{bmatrix}
		</md>.
		In this unique solution, <m>x_1 = 1</m> and <m>x_2 = 7</m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-inverses-solve-sys-3x3-1a">

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcrcr}
			 x \amp + \amp y \amp - \amp 3 z \amp =  3 \\
			   \amp   \amp y \amp - \amp 3 z \amp =  8 \\
			-x \amp + \amp y \amp - \amp 2 z \amp = -1
		\end{sysofeqns}
	</m></p></statement>

	<hint><p> First see <xref ref="exercise-inverses-as-system-3x3-invertible" text="type-local" />. </p></hint>

	<solution><p>
		This system is represented in matrix form <m> A \uvec{x} = \uvec{b} </m> where
		<md><mrow>
			A \amp =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				 1 \amp 1 \amp -3 \\
				 0 \amp 1 \amp -3 \\
				-1 \amp 1 \amp -2
			\end{abmatrix}
			\text{,}
			\amp
			\uvec{x} \amp = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix} \text{,} \amp
			\uvec{b} \amp = \begin{abmatrix}{r} 3 \\ 8 \\ -1 \end{abmatrix} \text{.}
		</mrow></md>
		The coefficient matrix is identical to the matrix of <xref ref="exercise-inverses-as-system-3x3-invertible" text="type-local" /> and has inverse
		<md>
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
				3 \amp -5 \amp 3 \\
				1 \amp -2 \amp 1
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>.
		The system therefore has unique solution
		<md>
			\uvec{x} =
			\inv{A} \uvec{b} =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
				3 \amp -5 \amp 3 \\
				1 \amp -2 \amp 1
			\end{abmatrix}
			\begin{abmatrix}{r} 3 \\ 8 \\ -1 \end{abmatrix}
			= \begin{abmatrix}{r} -5 \\ -34 \\ -14 \end{abmatrix}
		</md>.
		In this unique solution, <m>x = -5</m>, <m>y = -34</m>, and <m>z = -14</m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-inverses-solve-sys-3x3-1b">

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcrcr}
			 x_1 \amp + \amp x_2 \amp - \amp 3 x_3 \amp = 3 \\
			     \amp   \amp x_2 \amp - \amp 3 x_3 \amp = 5 \\
			-x_1 \amp + \amp x_2 \amp - \amp 2 x_3 \amp = 4
		\end{sysofeqns}
	</m></p></statement>

	<hint><p> First see <xref ref="exercise-inverses-as-system-3x3-invertible" text="type-local" />. </p></hint>

	<solution><p>
		This system has the same coefficient matrix as <xref ref="exercise-inverses-solve-sys-3x3-1a" text="type-local" />,
		but has variable and constant columns
		<md><mrow>
			\uvec{x} \amp = \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \end{bmatrix} \text{,}
			\amp
			\uvec{b} \amp = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 5 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix} \text{.}
		</mrow></md>
		We can use the same inverse matrix computed in <xref ref="exercise-inverses-as-system-3x3-invertible" text="type-local" /> to compute the unique solution
		<md>
			\uvec{x} =
			\inv{A} \uvec{b} =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
				3 \amp -5 \amp 3 \\
				1 \amp -2 \amp 1
			\end{abmatrix}
			\begin{abmatrix}{r} 3 \\ 5 \\ 4 \end{abmatrix}
			= \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ -4 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix}
		</md>.
		In this unique solution, <m>x_1 = -2</m>, <m>x_2 = -4</m>, and <m>x_3 = -3</m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
